Let me explain the most common and dangerous Spring AOP trap, the one that silently corrupts data, the way a senior developer would warn a junior.

First, `@Transactional` on a private method simply does not work, and Spring will not warn you. The reason lies in how Spring implements `@Transactional`. Spring does not rewrite your method. Instead it wraps your bean in a proxy, an invisible wrapper object. When some other object calls your service, it is really calling the proxy, and the proxy is the one that opens a transaction, then calls your real method, then commits. But a proxy can only intercept calls that come from outside the bean, and it can only wrap methods it can override, which means public methods. A private method is invisible to the proxy, so no transaction is ever started around it. Consider this broken code:

```java
@Service
public class SettlementService {
    public void process(Settlement s) {
        persist(s); // self-invocation — goes straight to the real method
    }

    @Transactional          // IGNORED: private + called via 'this'
    private void persist(Settlement s) {
        repo.save(s.getLeg1());
        repo.save(s.getLeg2()); // if this throws, leg1 is NOT rolled back
    }
}
```

Second, and closely related, is the self-invocation problem even for public methods. If a method inside the same bean calls another method using `this.method()`, that call never goes through the proxy — it goes straight to the real object. So even a public `@Transactional` method called by `this` from within the same bean has its annotation ignored. The same trap applies to `@Async`: a `@Async` method called via `this.sendNotification()` runs synchronously on the same thread, because the proxy that would have dispatched it to a background thread was bypassed.

The correct fixes are two. The clean, preferred fix is to extract the annotated method into a separate bean, so the call becomes a genuine external call that passes through that bean's proxy:

```java
@Service
public class SettlementService {
    private final SettlementPersister persister; // separate bean
    public void process(Settlement s) {
        persister.persist(s); // external call → proxy intercepts → transaction starts
    }
}

@Service
public class SettlementPersister {
    @Transactional
    public void persist(Settlement s) { /* now really transactional */ }
}
```

The alternative, less clean fix is to obtain the proxy of the current bean and call through it with `AopContext.currentProxy()`, which requires `exposeProxy = true`:

```java
((SettlementService) AopContext.currentProxy()).persist(s);
```

Third, context propagation across `@Async`. The security identity (`SecurityContext`) and the logging correlation id (`MDC`) are stored in `ThreadLocal`, which is tied to the current thread. When work jumps to a background thread via `@Async`, that new thread starts empty, so the user's identity and the trace id are lost. The fix is to carry the context across the thread boundary, for example by wrapping the executor with a `DelegatingSecurityContextExecutor` or by registering a `TaskDecorator` that copies the MDC:

```java
@Bean
public Executor asyncExecutor(ThreadPoolTaskExecutor base) {
    return new DelegatingSecurityContextExecutor(base); // carries SecurityContext
}
```

Fourth, serverless migration. When we want a small piece of banking logic to run as a serverless function, Spring Cloud Function lets us write a plain `Function` that takes an input and returns an output, with no web server, and deploy the same code to AWS Lambda or Azure Functions:

```java
@Bean
public Function<TradeRequest, TradeResponse> settleTrade() {
    return req -> new TradeResponse(req.getSymbol(), "SETTLED");
}
```

The senior-level lesson is that proxies only intercept external calls to overridable methods, so never put `@Transactional` or `@Async` on private methods or call them via `this`; extract them to another bean instead, and remember that `ThreadLocal` context must be propagated explicitly across async boundaries.

